{
  "markdown_report": "The best initial experiment is **explicitly weighted endpoint reconstruction**: control the contribution of endpoint prediction within one BERT4Rec model. Original BERT4Rec already mixes terminal masking with random reconstruction [1,2]; the proposed contribution is a controlled weighting experiment, not a new masking objective.\n\n**Insights and directions.** Three starting observations remain useful. First, feedback distinguishes broad relevance from a compelling next choice; endpoint emphasis is a testable hypothesis, not an established explanation. Second, absolute margins depend on score scale; candidate ranks better describe the pinned HR@10 objective. Third, target isolation and appended-mask readout require verification before interpreting failures.\n\nThree literature insights refine the experiment. BERT4Rec already includes endpoint-only examples [1,2]. Its released loss pools supervised targets, so example proportions do not directly determine objective weights [3]. Sampled ranking can disagree with full-catalog ranking [4]; retain the host evaluator and interpret diagnostics within their candidate sets. RankMe and shuffling studies remain complementary geometry and sensitivity tools [5,6], without establishing causes of ranking errors.\n\n**Framework.** Organize evidence along three axes: correctness → target isolation, reserved IDs and final-mask readout; task alignment → explicit loss coefficients, endpoint sampling and candidate ranks; representation use → order sensitivity and effective rank. This separates implementation failures, objective weighting and descriptive behavior without inferring ownership or intent.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/future potential/code difficulty, out of ten; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Explicit endpoint weighting (2/6/4):** separately average endpoint and reconstruction example losses, then combine them with α [1–3]. Originality: isolates a meaningful weighting choice, but reweights existing supervision. Potential: reproducible and extensible, but may not address compatibility. Difficulty: compact changes, but normalization and predictor consistency require care.\n- **Rank-based diagnostics (3/6/2):** measure positive rank, cutoff distance and hit indicators on fixed candidates [4]. Originality: useful alignment, but standard statistics. Potential: interpretable tracking, but no direct learning improvement. Difficulty: simple aggregation, but evaluator access must remain authorized.\n- **Paired recency sensitivity (4/6/4):** compare recent deletion with equal-count random deletion [6]. Originality: controlled adaptation, but established perturbation logic. Potential: informs hypotheses, but sensitivity does not establish preference. Difficulty: compact changes, but repeated controls cost inference.\n\n**Selected experiment.** Sample both objective groups explicitly. For endpoint examples, sample an eligible training user uniformly, then a target index uniformly among positions with preceding history; retain its latest L−1 preceding items and append MASK. Average each example’s supervised cross-entropies, then combine group means with α=0.5. Compare against pooled-target normalization using identical sampling, architecture and cross-entropy. This isolates weighting; any additional cold-start differences must be reported separately.\n\nKeep all four routes, seeds and training limits fixed, with one checkpoint per category. Select using host macro validation HR@10, recording every trial, validation query and GPU-second. Paired user bootstraps describe fixed-candidate uncertainty, not adaptive-selection bias [7,8]. Qualitative feedback remains hypothesis-generating; final generalization belongs to untouched host evaluation.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Test explicit weighting of two existing BERT4Rec supervision modes within each category’s single jointly trained model: endpoint-only reconstruction and ordinary masked reconstruction. Normalize supervised losses within each example, average within each explicitly sampled group, and combine group means using α=0.5. Endpoint masking itself is already part of original BERT4Rec.",
    "motivation": "At 0% research progress, a small, controlled weighting experiment is more justified than a new architecture. Under pooled-target normalization, an endpoint-example fraction p contributes approximately p/[p+(1−p)k] of target-loss weight when reconstruction examples average k targets. Explicit group coefficients remove this mask-count dependence, although they do not control gradient magnitudes. Feedback motivates investigating next-choice alignment but provides no evidence that objective weighting causes compatibility or intent failures.",
    "implementation_notes": "Start from the host’s corrected BERT4Rec architecture and preserve its reserved tokens, positional conventions, reconstruction corruption procedure and inference implementation. Before attributing results to weighting, document the supplied baseline’s endpoint sampling, corruption and loss normalization; these details cannot be verified from the provided diagnostic code. The controlled comparison uses the same two-group sampler and full-catalog item cross-entropy in both arms, changing only pooled-target versus explicit group normalization. If the cold start uses a different loss or sampler, report that discrepancy and do not attribute a direct cold-start comparison solely to weighting. Use the host batch size B, requiring B>=2, with floor(B/2) endpoint examples and the remainder reconstruction examples each step. Sample users uniformly from training users with at least two interactions; sample endpoint index t uniformly from 1,...,n−1 using zero-based indexing. Endpoint inputs contain only history[:t], truncated to its latest L−1 items, followed by MASK and left-padded to L. The target is history[t]. Preserve legitimate repeated IDs in preceding context. For the reconstruction group, use the corrected baseline’s existing training-only sampler and corruption policy unchanged, recording their settings and ensuring at least one supervised position per example. Compute cross-entropy over catalog IDs 1,...,itemnum, excluding PAD and MASK from the output classes; average over supervised positions within each example. Set L=0.5*mean(endpoint example losses)+0.5*mean(reconstruction example losses). Do not additionally weight examples by their group-sampling probability. The comparator sums the same target losses and divides by the total supervised-target count. Full-softmax cross-entropy needs no training negatives; retain correctly formed negative tensors for diagnostic-loader compatibility. Diagnostic negatives must exclude the position’s positive ID, PAD and MASK; do not consult validation or test labels to construct exclusions. Preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs and 200 batches per epoch, with existing optimizer and other settings held fixed across the controlled pair. Retain all four category routes, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts and one independently trained checkpoint per category at inference. Preserve item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items) and the host ModelView interfaces. DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader) must return metrics and metric_definitions; diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis) must use both. Probe only supervised masked positions, exclude PAD/MASK from geometry statistics and explicitly report empty measurements as unavailable. Never fabricate categories, reviews, ownership or simulator traits. Locate the final nonpadding position by maximizing valid position indices, never count_nonzero minus one. With left-padded appended-mask inputs it is the final sequence slot. Match context truncation, positional encoding, model class and readout in training, diagnosis and the embedded predictor writer; check prediction parity after checkpoint reload. Holding corrupted inputs fixed while changing labels must leave logits unchanged. Use host-provided validation ranks only when available through the authorized interface; a single diagnostic negative cannot reproduce 100-candidate HR@10. Do not suppress seen items in host candidate scoring, since repeated-item positives may be valid; retain the simulator’s separate unseen-item recommendation rule. Predeclare the controlled comparison and α=0.5, log all runs and failures, and stop after that comparison or when remaining budget cannot support its full cost. Select the highest host macro validation HR@10, retaining the corrected cold start on ties. If paired per-user validation outputs are available, bootstrap 10,000 times with seed 42, sampling 200 users with replacement within each category and using identical indices for both models; report the observed macro difference and percentile interval. This interval conditions on fitted models and fixed candidates and is exploratory after adaptive selection. No complete executable train.py can be specified faithfully from the supplied diagnostic description alone.",
    "pseudocode": "set preprocessing_seed=0; training_seed=42\nfor category in four_categories:\n    model = corrected_host_BERT4Rec()\n    for step in existing_schedule(max_epochs=200, batches_per_epoch=200):\n        endpoint_examples = []\n        for j in range(B // 2):\n            history = uniformly_sample_eligible_training_user_history()\n            t = seeded_rng.randint(1, len(history))  # upper bound exclusive\n            target = history[t]\n            context = history[:t][-(L-1):]\n            sequence = left_pad(context + [MASK], L)\n            labels = zeros(L)\n            labels[L-1] = target\n            endpoint_examples.append((sequence, labels))\n        reconstruction_examples = existing_corrected_reconstruction_sampler(B - B//2)\n        # Keep its corruption policy identical across comparison arms.\n        # Require at least one supervised position per example.\n        examples = endpoint_examples + reconstruction_examples\n        features = model.log2feats(stack_sequences(examples))\n        logits = score_catalog_ids_1_through_itemnum(features)\n        target_losses, valid_positions = unreduced_masked_cross_entropy(logits, labels)\n        per_example = sum_over_positions(target_losses) / count_supervised_positions(labels)\n        if arm == 'explicit_weighting':\n            loss = 0.5 * mean(per_example[:B//2]) + 0.5 * mean(per_example[B//2:])\n        else:  # matched comparator\n            loss = sum(target_losses) / sum(valid_positions)\n        optimizer_step(loss)\n    save_single_checkpoint_and_matching_predictor(category, model)\n    verify_reload_prediction_parity()\n# Host evaluates its unchanged validation users and 99 unique negatives.\n# Compare the controlled arms with the corrected cold start; select only by host macro HR@10.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 2,
      "positive": "Explicit normalization makes a potentially obscured BERT4Rec objective coefficient independently controllable and experimentally interpretable.",
      "negative": "Original BERT4Rec already includes endpoint-only masking, and weighted multitask losses are established techniques."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "A controlled result could support subsequent studies of reconstruction weighting across categories, history lengths and mask counts.",
      "negative": "Weighting alone may not resolve compatibility, duplication or present-intent failures; repeated validation tuning can also exaggerate apparent gains."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Preserves the BERT4Rec backbone and inference architecture, concentrating changes in sampling and loss reduction.",
      "negative": "Requires access to unreduced losses, a matched comparator, faithful reconstruction sampling, reserved-token handling and checkpoint/predictor parity checks."
    }
  },
